  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday Night through Thursday)\nIssued at 1119 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nMost of the Friday night period should be dry, although a modest\nincrease in shower and thunderstorm potential exists for parts of\nnortheast Oklahoma and northwest Arkansas toward daybreak\nSaturday given the amount of low level warm advection. This\npotential will continue through the day, although uncertainty\nremains high regarding coverage and location of the greatest\npotential. Forecast POPs on Saturday reflect a broadbrushed\nversion of a blend between the NBM and short-term blend. The highs\nfor Saturday have been left alone from the NBM given the\nuncertainty regarding how much rain and convective cloud cover\nwill be around during the day. Heat headlines will need to at\nleast be considered for parts of the area Saturday.\n\nAnother batch of showers and thunderstorms will approach the\nKansas border from the north Saturday night into early Sunday, but\nconfidence is higher that this will not move too far south into\nthe area given the building upper level high. Highs on Sunday and\nagain on Monday are likely to be pushing triple digits across a \ngood chunk of the area. The NBM initialization was largely\naccepted for highs both days, although some targeted increases\nwere applied to account for recent low biases at a few sites. Heat\nheadlines will likely be needed both days. Fire weather concerns \nwill increase once again on Sunday and especially on Monday due to\nthe return of gusty southerly winds. \n\nUnseasonably hot conditions will extend through at least the\nmiddle of next week, with only low chances for showers and\nthunderstorms during the period, focused across areas near the\nKansas border and the northern edge of the upper high. Uncertainty\nincreases thereafter, with continued signs of another front\napproaching the area late week into next weekend. The most recent\ndata indicates a later possible passage of this front than at this\nsame time yesterday.",
